<Base
  title="Identity and grants in sempods"
  description="How person identities, app identities and service credentials support delegated and service access, with grants resolved by the pod."
  schemaType="TechArticle"
>
  <p class="eyebrow">concepts · identity</p>
  <h1>Identity can travel. Authority stays with the pod.</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A useful open data space needs to recognize who is acting without making
    each app the owner of the person's identity. It also needs to distinguish
    an app acting for a person from a service acting as itself.
  </p>
  <h2>People have addressable identities</h2>
  <p>
    The current contract identifies a person through a WebID URI. A pod can
    attach grants to that identity. An optional OIDC bridge lets the person
    sign in through an upstream provider.
  </p>
  <p>
    The Kotlin identity service provides WebID profiles and that bridge.
    Trusted providers can assert equivalent WebID identities through the
    specified claim. The pod validates the assertion; similar-looking names
    do not establish that two identities belong to the same person.
  </p>
  <h2>Apps can act for a person</h2>
  <p>
    Delegated access uses Authorization Code with PKCE. The token identifies
    the person and client; the pod resolves the app's granted reach. An app
    receives the delegation the person approves within their own authority.
  </p>
  <div class="proof">
    <h3>A calendar can stay a calendar</h3>
    <p>
      A person may read events and private notes. They can let a calendar app
      read the event Context without sharing the notes. The app does not
      automatically receive everything its user can access.
    </p>
  </div>
  <h2>Services can act as themselves</h2>
  <p>
    A backend or scheduled worker uses Client Credentials. Its service identity
    and grants determine access; a person need not be online for every operation.
  </p>
  <p>
    Kotlin 0.2 adds experimental registration, owner consent and management for
    these services. The owner can choose their reach, narrow it or revoke it.
    Those setup extensions are described in the release's service-access guide.
  </p>
  <h2>Public data is still public</h2>
  <p>
    An anonymous caller can read public Contexts. Authenticated access can add
    private depth to the same resource. Each pod applies its own decisions;
    linking data does not establish a shared account or grant access elsewhere.
  </p>
